Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the average geometric mean of all $k$-element subsets is at most the average geometric mean of all $(k-1)$-element subsets. (The denominator $\binom{n}{k}$ confirms the sum ranges over all $\binom{n}{k}$ subsets of size $k$.)

**The inequality holds.** Here is the proof.

---

**Notation.** For a subset $S \subseteq [n]$ with $|S|=k$, let
$$f(S) = \left(\prod_{i \in S} a_i\right)^{1/k}, \qquad g(T) = \left(\prod_{i \in T} a_i\right)^{1/(k-1)} \text{ for } |T|=k-1.$$

Define $G_k = \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k}}\sum_{|S|=k} f(S)$ and $G_{k-1} = \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k-1}}\sum_{|T|=k-1} g(T)$. We prove $G_k \le G_{k-1}$.

---

**Key identity.** For any $k$-subset $S = \{i_1, \dots, i_k\}$, let $P = \prod_{j=1}^k a_{i_j}$ and $T_j = S \setminus \{i_j\}$. Then
$$\prod_{j=1}^k g(T_j) = \prod_{j=1}^k \left(\frac{P}{a_{i_j}}\right)^{1/(k-1)} = \left(\frac{P^k}{\prod_{j=1}^k a_{i_j}}\right)^{1/(k-1)} = \left(\frac{P^k}{P}\right)^{1/(k-1)} = P^{k-1 \over k-1} = P = f(S)^k.$$

Therefore $f(S) = \left(\prod_{j=1}^k g(T_j)\right)^{1/k}$.

---

**Applying AM-GM.** By the AM-GM inequality applied to the $k$ nonnegative values $\{g(T_j)\}_{j=1}^k$:
$$f(S) = \left(\prod_{j=1}^k g(T_j)\right)^{1/k} \le \frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^k g(T_j) = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{i \in S} g(S \setminus \{i\}).$$

---

**Summing over all $k$-subsets.**
$$\sum_{|S|=k} f(S) \;\le\; \frac{1}{k}\sum_{|S|=k}\sum_{i \in S} g(S \setminus \{i\}).$$

In the double sum on the right, each $(k-1)$-subset $T$ is counted once for every $i \in [n] \setminus T$, i.e., exactly $n - (k-1) = n-k+1$ times (since $S = T \cup \{i\}$ ranges over all $k$-subsets containing $T$). Thus:
$$\sum_{|S|=k} f(S) \;\le\; \frac{n-k+1}{k}\sum_{|T|=k-1} g(T).$$

---

**Normalizing.** Divide both sides by $\binom{n}{k}$:
$$G_k \;\le\; \frac{n-k+1}{k\,\binom{n}{k}}\sum_{|T|=k-1} g(T).$$

Using the identity $\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n-k+1}{k}\binom{n}{k-1}$, we get $\frac{n-k+1}{k\,\binom{n}{k}} = \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k-1}}$, so:
$$G_k \;\le\; \frac{1}{\binom{n}{k-1}}\sum_{|T|=k-1} g(T) \;=\; G_{k-1}.$$

---

**Conclusion.** The inequality
$$\frac{\displaystyle\sum_{\text{cyc}} \sqrt[k]{a_1 \cdots a_k}}{\binom{n}{k}} \;\le\; \frac{\displaystyle\sum_{\text{cyc}} \sqrt[k-1]{a_1 \cdots a_{k-1}}}{\binom{n}{k-1}}$$
holds for all positive reals $a_1, \dots, a_n$ and all integers $2 \le k \le n$. Equality holds if and only if $a_1 = a_2 = \cdots = a_n$ (since AM-GM achieves equality iff all $g(T_j)$ are equal, which propagates to all $a_i$ being equal).

